Writer: a large DOCX made of paragraphs and simple tables is laid out page by page, as large plain texts are.

DOCX has no layout cache, so every paragraph and table of a newly opened document is created on the first
page. Each page break then moves the whole rest of the document to the next page (SwFlowFrame::MoveFwd:
GetNextLeaf walks the following frames, PasteTree reparents them), O(paragraphs x pages). A 5 MB DOCX of
paragraphs and small tables (9857 pages) spent 686 s of CPU in its full layout, half of it in MoveFwd, against
118 s for 2 MB (3938 pages); 50 MB never finished. Saving as DOCX runs the full layout first, so the same
time was spent again on every save.

hst-sw-large-text-pagination.patch already inserts pages while the frames are created for plain texts,
from a generous height estimate per paragraph, and lets the layout pull the following frames back, so
the final pagination is that of the start-to-end layout. It now also runs for a DOCX document of at least
3000 nodes without header, footer, columns, footnotes, frames, images or drawing objects
(HstLargeDocxPaginationAllowed). For such a document, tables are estimated as well: each row is as tall as its
tallest cell, estimated paragraph by paragraph on the cell's share of the body width, plus the cell
borders and spacing, and at least the row's own height (HstEstimateTableHeight). Paragraph text in a larger
font set on the characters (DOCX run properties) is estimated in that font (HstMaxCharHeight). The estimate
stops, and the layout continues on its own as before, at a table it does not cover (nested tables, split
cells, repeated headings), a section, or a change of page format.

Appending the estimated pages was itself O(pages) per page: SwPageFrame::Paste walked all pages to
find the end (SwFrame::InsertBefore without sibling), and PreparePage formatted the new page, whose
PrepareMake walks all pages before it. A 50 MB DOCX (about 100,000 pages) spent most of its open time
there. While the estimate inserts a page (SwRootFrame::IsHstEstimatedPageInsert), the page is appended
behind the root's last page, and is formatted by the layout like its frames instead of right away.
Formatting a page (SwFrame::PrepareMake) walked all pages before it to format the unformatted ones,
O(pages) for every page of the layout. For such documents it starts after the last formatted page before
it (HstFirstSiblingToFormat); the unformatted pages before that are formatted by the layout in page order.
These two also apply to the large plain texts of hst-sw-large-text-pagination.patch.

Hook: sw/source/core/layout/laycache.cxx (filter check, paragraph estimate split from the frame
dispatch, page insert flag), sw/source/core/layout/layhelp.hxx, sw/source/core/inc/rootfrm.hxx,
sw/source/core/layout/pagechg.cxx (Paste, PreparePage), sw/source/core/layout/calcmove.cxx (PrepareMake),
sw/Library_sw.mk; logic in
sw/source/core/inc/hstlargedocxpagination.hxx, sw/source/core/layout/hstlargedocxpagination.cxx.
Test: CppunitTest_sw_core_layout testLargeDocxPaginationUnchanged (every page start equal to the
start-to-end layout, and no page left holding the rest of the document after loading),
testOtherFormatsNotPaginated (DOCX now paginated, ODT not).
